{
 "id": "M11",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "Both batches agree: the 0 to 100 scale offers more cutoffs",
 "section": "appendix",
 "order": 112,
 "caption": "One monitor at a time, with both problem batches overlaid. Each distinct honest score is one cutoff: x is the share of honest programs audited, y the backdoors caught. Filled marks are the original 500, hollow marks the second 500. The dashed line is a 5% audit share.",
 "notes": [
  "The compact cut of M09 and M10, for when the point is that the two batches tell the same story rather than how each one looks.",
  "Orange sits left of and above blue in both batches, which is the whole claim: a wider score scale puts more operating points within reach, including cheaper ones.",
  "Hollow marks the second batch rather than a dashed line, because a dashed line in this project means a threshold and nothing else.",
  "Sonnet's 0 to 10 arm on batch 2 appears as a single hollow blue mark. That is not a drawing error: the arm produced 2 distinct honest scores and so one usable cutoff."
 ],
